### Existing habit-tracker pattern

I followed the existing Habits architecture: the registry entry in [tools.ts](src/renderer/src/tools.ts) supplies sidebar and routing metadata; [Habits.tsx](src/renderer/src/modules/Habits.tsx) and its editor handle the UI; SQLite schema versions are appended in [migrations.ts](src/main/migrations.ts) and applied at startup; main-process queries are exposed through IPC and preload; the dashboard and review assemble summaries; and shared rules are covered by focused tests.

### Task Manager added

- Added the SQLite task and subtask tables with a versioned migration, local-calendar due dates, and backup/restore support.
- Implemented task editing, quick-add, filters and views, native drag-to-reorder, keyboard shortcuts, and recurring task creation. The UI uses the app’s theme classes.
- Registered Tasks under Productivity; added “New task” and “Go to Today’s tasks” palette actions, a dashboard summary, review counts, and a Quick Capture Task type.
- Kept this pass to the **list view**; there is no Kanban board yet. No dependencies were added.

Key files: [Tasks.tsx](src/renderer/src/modules/Tasks.tsx), [TaskEditor.tsx](src/renderer/src/modules/TaskEditor.tsx), [task rules](src/shared/tasks.ts), [database queries](src/main/tasks.ts), [task tests](tests/tasks.ts), and [migration tests](tests/migrations.ts).

**Verification passed:** `npm test`, `npm run typecheck`, and `npm run build`. The tests cover recurrence, date filters, migration safety, and related integrations.

A natural next step would be adding a Kanban board that shares the task data and filters.

Another option is to improve task navigation with search and bulk actions.
